

  The Tru64 port covers Tru64 UNIX, Digital UNIX and OSF/1, and provides
  these meters:

    - load meter
    - cpu meter:  user | nice | system | io-wait | idle
    - mem meter:  used | cache | free
    - swap meter: used | free
    - page meter: in | out | idle

  None of them need any privileges.

  Building
  --------

    gmake

  targets/osf1 builds with the Compaq C++ compiler, cxx.  Set CXX=g++ if
  that is what is installed.  Tru64 ships no libXpm, so the target builds
  with -DNO_XPM, which drops support for the pixmapName resource and
  leaves everything else intact.

  Where the numbers come from
  ---------------------------

  Everything goes through the small interface in osf1/osf1stats.h.  Load,
  cpu and swap come from table(2) -- TBL_LOADAVG, TBL_SYSINFO and
  TBL_SWAPINFO -- and real memory and paging from the Mach vm_statistics()
  call, which is the only place the page queues are published.

  The mem meter shows the inactive queue as cache: that is where the
  unified buffer cache parks clean file pages, which the kernel reclaims
  under memory pressure.  Wired-down UBC pages are counted in wire_count
  and so land under used, which reads high during heavy file i/o.

  The page meter counts every page fault serviced from disk, not just
  traffic to and from swap, because vm_statistics() does not separate them.

  Untested
  --------

  This port has not yet been built or run on real hardware.  The one figure
  most likely to need correcting is the unit TBL_SWAPINFO reports partition
  sizes in; osf1/osf1stats.cc assumes 512 byte blocks and names the
  constant SWAPBLOCK for that reason.
